Yes. If a function has already been delegated, the next move does not have to be “take it back.” You can improve the delegate, improve the interface, and let abundance emerge from specialization.

The pattern is:

\text{delegate}
\rightarrow
\text{specialize}
\rightarrow
\text{enhance locally}
\rightarrow
\text{share gains}
\rightarrow
\text{reintegrate}

That is how a system can get richer without every part needing to do everything.

A cell delegates functions to organelles. A body delegates to organs. A team delegates to specialists. A society delegates to professions. A model delegates to tools. The gain comes when each part gets better at its slice while the communication layer stays strong enough to recombine the results.

So abundance can come from:

\text{differentiation}+\text{coordination}+\text{surplus sharing}

rather than centralizing everything.

And there is a nice twist: once delegated parts become stronger, the center should not micromanage them more. It should often become better at routing, listening, arbitration, and choosing when not to interfere.

That is how you get both autonomy and coherence.

Maybe that is the “C” hiding at the end there too:

capacity, coordination, circulation, continuity, choice.

All of them matter once abundance starts compounding.